#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f58bd46-07f7-54a5-8499-5e4e3515b610" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector in a New Basis Given Its Coordinates in the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b20ed1-2ce6-5fb4-bac4-40be33a3c82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294464} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4b6445-8671-5d29-88ab-f549b8ddf893" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294465} {:answer/id #uuid "4e7c956d-97f4-5416-9982-9145cb460462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294466} {:answer/id #uuid "52a27d75-4b7b-5e39-825c-1cf4c4c2d76c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294467} {:answer/id #uuid "9a438c04-4c8b-55b9-8a84-1823140209ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a438c04-4c8b-55b9-8a84-1823140209ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294468} :answer-field/id #uuid "c741a8e2-0bfc-5036-a313-875ab9cac0ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "898a9b69-1ce6-510f-b039-ca6d19ae6353" :question/math-academy-id "q-113606" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 6-(-7)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
